---
description: Answer questions without editing; do not call a fix done without evidence; stop circling after repeated failed attempts; clean up debug instrumentation
alwaysApply: true
---

## Questions are not change requests

- Answer questions ("what causes...", "why does...", "is there...", "verify that..."); propose a
  change if useful. Edit files only when the user asks for a change.
- "Report findings" / "make no changes" means read-only for the whole task, follow-ups included,
  until the user says otherwise.

## Prove it before calling it fixed

- Call a bug fixed only after **reproducing the original failure** and showing it **gone** (test,
  script, log line). For UI and performance work, give the **measured number** (ms per drag, s per
  control point, peak RSS) before and after.
- If you could not run the reproduction (needs the GUI, real data, a server), say so, give the exact
  steps to confirm it, and do not write "fixed".

## Stop circling

- After **two** failed attempts at the same symptom, stop editing: list hypotheses with evidence for
  and against each, and pick the next measurement that separates them. Ask the user if that evidence
  needs their eyes or data.
- Before a new implementation, find the **existing sibling** doing the same job (another trainer,
  TEM vs SEM path, Windows vs WSL/Linux script), mirror its structure, and update every counterpart
  that must change with it.

## Temporary instrumentation

- Gate debug logging, dump folders, and timing probes behind an environment variable or the logger
  level; never on by default.
- Remove them and their output folders before committing unless the user asks to keep them; list any
  kept in the commit message.
